<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="android:editTextBackground">@drawable/rn_edit_text_material</item>
        <!--
          React Native measures text with a TextPaint of its own and draws it in a TextView.
          Android 15 changed what that TextView does by default for apps targeting API 35+, and
          React Native was never told: the string "elegant" does not appear anywhere in its Android
          source, so its measuring paint keeps the compact metrics while the view it hands the text
          to paints with the taller elegant ones. Every line then draws a little taller than it was
          measured, the deficit accumulates down the block, and the last line falls outside the
          view. It is worst with emoji and other tall glyphs, which is where the two metric sets
          differ most. Restoring the pre-15 defaults makes measuring and drawing agree again.
          https://github.com/facebook/react-native/issues/53286 (fix landed in 0.84, then reverted)
        -->
        <item name="android:textViewStyle">@style/TextViewStyle</item>
        <item name="editTextStyle">@style/EditTextStyle</item>
        <item name="android:editTextStyle">@style/EditTextStyle</item>
    </style>

    <!-- Attributes unknown to a platform are ignored there, so these are inert below API 35. -->
    <style name="TextViewStyle" parent="Widget.AppCompat.TextView">
        <item name="android:elegantTextHeight">false</item>
        <item name="android:useBoundsForWidth">false</item>
        <item name="android:shiftDrawingOffsetForStartOverhang">false</item>
    </style>

    <style name="EditTextStyle" parent="Widget.AppCompat.EditText">
        <item name="android:elegantTextHeight">false</item>
        <item name="android:useLocalePreferredLineHeightForMinimum">false</item>
        <!--
          The same API-35 change as TextViewStyle above, and the reason it was missed here is the
          reason it is visible: React Native hands a TextInput to the platform's own EditText and
          never lays the text out itself, so the fix it shipped for Text does not reach it. The
          EditText keeps the new default, which sizes and offsets the line from the glyphs' visual
          bounds instead of their advances.

          What that costs depends on the left padding. Every other field in the app carries its own
          paddingHorizontal, so the first glyph starts far enough in that the offset has room. The
          composer is the one field whose padding sits on the wrapper (the frame moved there on
          2026-08-18, so an EditText past its ceiling would stop scrolling its padding away), which
          leaves its text at x=0: the first glyph, drawn at a slightly negative x, is simply cut.
          Reported as "l'input mange la première lettre", on one line as much as on six.
          Restoring the pre-15 behaviour puts the advances back in charge, as for Text.
        -->
        <item name="android:useBoundsForWidth">false</item>
        <item name="android:shiftDrawingOffsetForStartOverhang">false</item>
    </style>

</resources>
